5ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106291" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +3,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\frac{1}{2},\\,y=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2},\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(x-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{5}(y+\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{1}{25}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{25}{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is ${(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e404b38bbdf5c756f675cf0dfcc2c4906bcd2d62b1b4d277b8fc0cfb85e9b2d4" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=\\sqrt{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106300" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{(y+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}=25"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/state.json" "file_sha256" "be160248e907dc0090c0e85ac5f1243f82e74a4127fd354d2039dad9e67a4fd7" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/156d160267551e9dbf4db42b14b1e0dbb46ab658a836a760cf31a42eb89662d3.png)" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-5374.json" "file_sha256" "98882032db6c880a8c2b09f3462f0604712226834197d8e05e5f636dd4341861" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5374" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-5374.json" "file_sha256" "98882032db6c880a8c2b09f3462f0604712226834197d8e05e5f636dd4341861" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "c4d1d0c059fe978e74cd0dcfaf09cc1a8b5101d407b5d8ec86608f7b77d3f92b" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "c4d1d0c059fe978e74cd0dcfaf09cc1a8b5101d407b5d8ec86608f7b77d3f92b" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e" "basis" 1075 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $15$ centered at the origin?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "76ff91c362bc69ed73552b3800976ced33256fa2cc8363dcf93b3d2ad10af222" "basis" 1312 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=15.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=15\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=15\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/state.json" "file_sha256" "76ff91c362bc69ed73552b3800976ced33256fa2cc8363dcf93b3d2ad10af222" "basis" 1312 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-105915" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file"