047d554af"} {"question" "q-79270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a semi-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/9f515c8b70ee6902df4c694f7d7b50efb4ff54d24b0cb25b9173d439fea84210.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\pi ]$, we get the required semi-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the semi-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79270" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "08a0af05137b3b688e49841fbd36085d8f8eb79fe4c5a4766992caf1adb2f92e"} {"question" "q-79270" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da8