cle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [\\pi ,2\\pi ].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-275161" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "08a0af05137b3b688e49841fbd36085d8f8eb79fe4c5a4766992caf1adb2f92e"} {"question" "q-275161" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-275161" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-275161" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-275161" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\pi"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/c9e8fb87758eefb1facfea08112c5add0cc54d481e36e740d3cc623f05fa41fb.png)\n\nThe arc of a circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=2.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to the arc above, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/8a0491010e71d24ebac54b5b8d03b1ce45f7de90a0f21ff1ea2f0c2babf4ec77.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]$, we get the required arc of the circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the arc of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "08a0af05137b3b688e49841fbd36085d8f8eb79fe4c5a4766992caf1adb2f92e"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{5\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}]"] ["math" "\\theta \\in [\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-79165" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-106299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Determine the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1,\\,y=\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2,\\,0\\le \\theta <2\\pi .\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-106299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta .$ We can do this using the Pythagorean theorem.\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(x+1) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{3}(y+2) & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{9}{(x+1)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{9}{(y+2)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ 4{(x+1)}^{2}+4{(y+2)}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation of the circle is ${(x+1)}^{2}+{(y+2)}^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-106299" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/activity-metadata.json