theta $ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+3 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(x+3) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}(y-1) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations and using ${cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta =1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{4}{25}{(x+3)}^{2}+\\frac{4}{25}{(y-1)}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian equation is ${(x+3)}^{2}+{(y-1)}^{2}=\\frac{25}{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-321988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "08a0af05137b3b688e49841fbd36085d8f8eb79fe4c5a4766992caf1adb2f92e"} {"question" "q-321988" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/state.json" "file_sha256" "e73d7fb0bde6cf197d39bc710757072854b7c3a5f490c2e36da885f047d554af"} {"question" "q-321988" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_successful