 a circle  of radius $r$ centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nUnder this parametrization, the circle is traversed once in the counterclockwise direction, starting from $(r,0).$\n\nIn our case, the radius is $r = 2.$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are \n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-5374.json" "file_sha256" "84aafc126fc0cc6838ad2f0b7f706028d9ce9f934f251c3cff5c7c29f92b1579"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that generates a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1"} {"question" "e-5375" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle of radius $r$ centered at the point $(a,b)$ are\n\n$$\nx= r\\cos\\theta+a,\\quad y=r\\sin\\theta+b,\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have a circle of radius $2$ centered at the point $(4,6).$ Therefore, the parametric equations of the circle are\n\n$$\nx=2\\cos\\theta+4,\\quad y=2\\sin\\theta+6,\\quad\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-5375.json" "file_sha256" "9438d2ed187a6532762909d465a4b1438ffc5fadc886b7adff4895efa35b14c1"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/f44fad12ad1f7d465423f92258caae38e0c536153ba2c3c3a6de573061c69993.png)\n\nThe quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx = 6\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = 6\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "bb2d0ddb0aed16df81f8c7ef8871d67af1bb44c0fc1f7d0f0008517e15ce2068"} {"question" "e-6779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx = r\\cos\\theta, \\quad y = r\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r = 6.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta$ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/assets/4bd384e2b1a9d0568617ddbfd5ac7dbe9630882b45c40d7912f4809a6e0be551.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta$ to $\\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right],$ we get the required quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=6\\cos\\theta,\\quad y=6\\sin\\theta, \\quad \\theta \\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-6779.json" "file_sha256" "bb2d0ddb0aed16df81f8c7ef8871d67af1bb44c0fc1f7d0f0008517e15ce2068"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$$\nx=3\\cos\\theta + 2,\\quad y=3\\sin\\theta - 4, \\quad 0\\leq\\theta\\lt 2\\pi.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e"} {"question" "e-6817" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the circle, we need to eliminate the parameter $\\theta.$ We can do this using the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\cos^2 \\theta + \\sin^2 \\theta = 1\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\cos\\theta$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy isolating the $\\sin\\theta$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+4}{3} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & ={sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the Pythagorean identity, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{9} & =1 \\\\ (x-2)^{2}+(y+4)^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the Cartesian equation is $(x-2)^2 + (y+4)^2 = 9.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039602/example-6817.json" "file_sha256" "66dc5674cde4a9a81d17e1f9471090bf3f2bda4f507f4b4799fb52c93c87fc3e"}] "usage" {"q-79170" [] "q-321891" [] "q-6878" [] "e-6779" [] "q-106300" [] "q-106051" [] "q-78204" [] "q-79166" [] "e-5375" [] "q-6879" [] "q-105915" [] "e-6817" [] "q-106291" [] "e-5374" []} "basis" 1374 "retractions" [[17592186299498 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/da3a33a3ade10663064516bac22c07459ccf7ce556753ba94d70ee832e947458.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $10$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(10,0).$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,y=$\n{{field-2}}$,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$"] [17592186285483 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/de2b66f976dc204566516a1724fa19cc03bf8b8f4da1fa4f4a198fd656ad10ca.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(9,0)?$"] [17592186088298 :question/problem "Which of the following parametric equations, defined for $\\theta  \\in  [0, 2\\pi ),$ generate a circle of radius $1$ centered at the point $(3, 2)$?"] [17592186285409 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/3c07f3e732084327f02849cee9355a46ce7a1c5f905d509b1e1404915612c85c.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $3$ centered at $(3,-2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(6,-2)?$"] [17592186285497 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13997706/assets/1407fa82fd00d07311cb34c6a3d5f0d0c79808d321d049d74114a6f4f2e5f6a6.png)\n\nConsider the circle of radius $2$ centered at $(3,2)$, as shown above. Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ),$  traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(5,2)?$"] [17592186299543 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/5ac5a6ba5e7c041adfe2a36f9e7d835e8090a296cb72adffa5123f67cbed8aa3.png)\n\nThe three-quarter-circle above is defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $\\theta ?$"] [17592186299543 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a circle  of radius $r$  centered at  the origin are\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, the radius $r=3.$ So, the parametric equations of the *full* circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a three-quarter-circle, we associate the parameter $\\theta $ with the central angle formed by a point $(x,y)$ on the circle and the $x$-axis. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14038487/assets/6f27674dd7382f9040cdeb8e85ea35ff63e581ef5752b373aa0ab87a0b3e2f0f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $\\theta $ to $[0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}]$, we get the required three-quarter-circle.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the three-quarter-circle are \n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\theta \\in [0,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}].\n$$"] [17592186088216 :question/problem "Calculate the Cartesian equation of the circle\n\n$x = 3\\cos \\theta , y = 3\\sin \\theta , 0 \\le \\theta  < 2\\pi $."] [17592186087489 :question/problem "Consider the circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown below. Construct a set of parametric equations, defined for $\\theta\\in[0,2\\pi),$ that traverse the circle once in the counterclockwise direction starting at the point $A(2,0).$ \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/referen